  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1202 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nMidday satellite imagery depicted cirrus increasing rapidly\nahead of an approaching shortwave centered near the Four Corners\nat noon. Cold 1032 mb high pressure ridging that brought the\nfall season's first widespread killing freeze this morning, is\nstarting to weaken and retreat eastward per noon surface\nobservations. Many locations fell into the upper teens across\neastern zones this morning, closest to this ridge axis and in\nthe strongest radiational cooling. Return flow is establishing\nat midday, and southeast winds will continue to increase \nthrough this afternoon, particularly west of US 83. Southeast \nwind gusts to near 35 mph are expected west of US 83 before \nsunset. Southeast winds are only recirculating the chilly air, \nwith 850 mb temperatures constrained to -1 or -2C. Combined with\nincreasing cirrus this afternoon, most locations will not warm \nout of the lower 40s. \n\nClouds will continue to thicken and lower tonight as the\nshortwave approaches, and this added blanket should keep\ntemperatures several degrees less cold Tuesday morning, in the\nupper 20s. Incoming shortwave is progressive and starved for\nmoisture, and not much is expected from it. Weak lift will be\nfocused on the western zones - west of US 83 - from about\nmidnight tonight through mid morning Tuesday. Here, scattered\nsnow showers are expected, where some minor accumulations\n(dusting to near one inch) are probable. QPF/snow amount grids\nare focused on the western 3 counties (Hamilton/Stanton/Morton)\nwhere minor grassy accumulations will be focused. Some minor\ntravel impacts with slick bridges and overpasses are expected in\nthese counties for the Tuesday morning commute. NBM probability\nof snowfall > 1 inch is still restricted to about 30% across\nthese western 3 counties - most locations will receive much\nless. Flurries may reach as far east as US 83 Tuesday morning,\nbefore lift ends and the sky clears rapidly Tuesday afternoon.\nEven with afternoon sunshine, 850 mb temperatures are forecast\nto remain just below 0C, with afternoon highs only near 40. \n\nThe clearing sky sets the table for another widespread hard\nfreeze Wednesday morning in the upper teens and lower 20s. \n\nAnother dry midlevel shortwave sinking south down the Missouri\nvalley Wednesday will keep cool high pressure in control over\nKansas, which will in turn keep afternoon temperatures chilly in\nthe 40s. Thanksgiving will be quiet and beautiful, with\ntemperatures not far from late November normals, sunshine and\nvery light winds. Strong 1033 mb surface high over SW KS will\nprovide another widespread strong freeze Thanksgiving morning,\nonce again in the upper teens and lower 20s. Moderating\nafternoon temperatures are forecast Friday and Saturday, back \nto the upper 50s and lower 60s. The next dry cold front is\nexpected over the upcoming weekend with a northerly wind shift,\nbut the dry forecast will continue with no impacts.",
